// `@[convention(…, attach = StructName, order = N)]` (issue #2178, split
// from #2164's 2026-08-03 design-backport comment "item 2") end to end.
//
// `cue`'s declared return type (`: Cue`) must agree with its `attach = Cue`
// clause or the declaration is `E180` and never registered as a claiming
// handler at all — this fixture proves the AGREEING case compiles and runs.
//
// ⚠ UPDATED (issue #2108, `docs/decision-log.md` 2026-08-03 "The element
// output model"): `cue`'s claimed `VENDOR` line no longer becomes its own
// visible transcript line — an attaching convention CONSUMES its own line
// (ruling item 2/6: "AN EVENT EXISTS IFF A LINE EXISTS"). `cue`'s `Cue`
// value's fields (`speaker`, `voiceover`) are merged into the VM's
// per-block attachment state instead, and every line materialized while
// they're live carries a COPY (ruling item 5) — see `crates/brink-runtime/
// tests/element.rs`'s `attach_convention_data_reaches_the_following_run`
// for the actual `Element.data` assertion this fixture's own text-only
// transcript can't express. `ClaimHandlerDecl::attach` now has that one
// real consumer (the runtime's `Opcode::AttachElement`); the editor/host
// projection (`#2111`) is still unbuilt.
struct Cue {
  speaker: string,
  voiceover: bool,
}

@[convention(claims = "^(?<name>[A-Z][A-Z '-]*)$", attach = Cue, order = 10)]
fn cue(name: string): Cue {
  return Cue { speaker: name, voiceover: false };
}

flow main() {
  VENDOR
  You shouldn't be here after dark.
  -> END
}
